{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high versus realized prices (often by ~5–12 €/t), especially beyond ~day 10. Apply a downward level shift to the whole path, increasing with horizon.",
    "Missed regime shift / mean reversion: when recent history shows a peak/spike (e.g., ~72–73) followed by weakening, the model extrapolates elevated levels instead of reverting. Add a mean-reversion pull toward the recent 30d average (or a lower post-break mean) starting around days 5–10.",
    "Horizon-dependent overshoot: short-horizon (days 1–5) errors are small, but mid/long horizon (days 10–30) errors grow sharply as the model stays near 72–75 while reality trends down to mid-60s. Use a horizon-scaled damping factor that compresses deviations from the anchor mean as horizon increases.",
    "Trend sign error after turning points: after a local top in history, the model continues forecasting flat-to-up; realized path trends down. If the last 5–10 days show decelerating gains or a rollover, force forecast drift negative for the next 2–3 weeks.",
    "Overreaction to recent highs: forecasts cluster tightly around recent highs (low dispersion) while outcomes show sustained selloff. Introduce an asymmetric correction: after upward spikes, cap upside continuation and increase probability/size of downward moves (stronger than after downward spikes)."
  ]
}
